# One file per game, default-exporting the game object. The database never learns what games
# exist -- it refers to them by bare `game_id` with no foreign key
# (ADR-game-content-lives-on-disk), so RENAMING A GAME'S `id` ORPHANS EVERY ROW ALREADY
# WRITTEN AGAINST IT.
#
# Shared by every kind:
#
#   id, title      identity, and the words on the tile
#   hero           the top half of the page: { text } OR { asset, alt }, never both
#   blurb          a sentence UNDER the hero, for the one case the kit forbids inside it --
#                  a picture that still needs words
#   starter        true if the tile is open for every team from onboarding, with no code. Only
#                  where learning about it late is unrecoverable (#7). A hunt may not be one.
#   hints          revealed one at a time, priced by content/economy.js. The first is free.
#   unfinished()   returns the reasons this game is not finished; boot warns loudly for each.
#                  Same bargain as `pending: true` in codes.js -- the hole is loud in the log
#                  and honest on the page, and never stops the site coming up.
#
# answer -- hero + form, ONE submission per team, editable until game end
#   { id, title, kind: 'answer', hero, form: { placeholder, inputmode }, hints: [],
#     check(value, ctx) {},           // judged on submit; return true/false/points
#     resolve(submissions, ctx) {} }  // OR judged at game end across every team, returning
#                                     // [{ teamId, submissionId, points, verdict, reason }]
#   It spends its share of the tile budget inside check/resolve, where boot cannot check the
#   arithmetic -- so the ticket that writes the game owns it. See yarn.js.
#
# tally -- hero + form, MANY submissions (one point per photo)
#   { id, title, kind: 'tally', points, hero, photo: true, judging: 'trust' | 'manual', hints: [] }
#
# hunt -- ordered steps, no form. Banks PER STEP as each is reached, so the points live on the
# step and a game-level `points` is a boot error.
#   { id, title, kind: 'hunt',
#     steps: [{ hero: { text }, hints: [], webhook: 'hallway-lights', points: 2 }] }
#   Every step needs exactly one slug in content/codes.js, or boot fails.
#
# trophy -- a physical object in the house: hero and blurb, no form, no steps, no judging. The
# host awards it by hand at /admin/game/:id, so it must declare `points` -- the award button has
# to print a number and nothing later in the night can work one out. See teddy.js.
#   { id, title, kind: 'trophy', points, hero, blurb }
